column44:
Main Similarities
both concern audio frame counts;
both are useful for audio buffer sizing;
both are relevant to real-time/low-latency audio processing;
both expose the processing granularity in sample frames;
both can help a developer determine how much audio data is processed per I/O cycle.

Apple explicitly states that I/O buffer size is expressed in sample frames and affects the rate at which the audio stack performs I/O.

Main Residual Differences
HarmonyOS explicitly describes frames per callback.
macOS exposes device I/O buffer frame size.
The exact callback delivery behavior is not guaranteed to be identical merely because the buffer frame size matches.
macOS uses an AudioObjectPropertyAddress and Core Audio property mechanism rather than a capturer-specific getter.
Depending on the macOS architecture used, an application may additionally need Audio Unit configuration to establish the exact processing relationship.

