Main Similarities
both control an active audio stream;
both can pause recording;
both are low-level/native audio APIs;
both operate on persistent audio-stream objects;
both distinguish pause from stopping/disposal;
both support continuation of the audio workflow.

Apple explicitly describes Audio Queue Services as a C API for recording/playback and AudioQueuePause as pausing recording or playback.

Main Residual Differences
AudioQueue supports both recording and playback, whereas the HarmonyOS API is specifically an audio capturer;
Apple's queue has its own buffer/callback lifecycle;
HarmonyOS has its own OH_AudioCapturer state/result model;
exact buffering, hardware routing, and session semantics differ;
platform audio-session configuration remains platform-specific.

These are meaningful implementation differences, but they do not destroy the core use-case equivalence.

column26:
Main Similarities
native object lifecycle management;
release of resources associated with an object;
called when the application no longer needs the object;
relevant to multimedia native objects;
terminal/release-oriented lifecycle role.
Main Residual Differences
OH_AudioCodec_Destroy explicitly destroys an audio codec instance;
IUnknown::Release decrements a COM reference count;
actual COM object destruction occurs only when the reference count reaches zero;
Release is generic across COM objects rather than audio-codec-specific;
Windows codec implementations may have their own lifecycle requirements in addition to COM reference management.

column23:
OH_AuxiliaryPictureNative_Create creates an auxiliary-picture object from pixel data,
 while GetColorContext retrieves the default color context of a pixel format; 
they serve fundamentally different purposes.
